Fetching an API means sending a request to a server and getting data back
Frontend → Request → Server/API → Response → UI Update

Using fetch() + async/await

We usually fetch API inside useEffect
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchData = async () => {
      const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/data");
      const result = await res.json();
      setData(result);
    };

    fetchData();
  }, []);

  return <div>{data.length}</div>;
}
GET → fetch data
POST → send data
PUT/PATCH → update
DELETE → remove

API fetching involves sending an HTTP request from the frontend to a server and receiving a response, usually in JSON format. In React, I typically use fetch or async/await inside useEffect to call APIs and store the response in state, which then updates the UI dynamically

Bootstrap is a component-based CSS framework that provides pre-designed UI elements, allowing faster development. Tailwind CSS is a utility-first framework that offers low-level classes for building custom designs. Bootstrap is easier for quick projects, while Tailwind provides greater flexibility and is preferred for modern, highly customized interfaces